{"title":"Contrasting the Interaction Structure of an Email and a Telephone Corpus: A Machine Learning Approach to Annotation of Dialogue Function Units","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W09-3953/","doi":"10.3115/1708376.1708429","authors":["Jun Hu","Rebecca Passonneau","Owen Rambow"],"year":2009,"abstract":"Presents a dialogue annotation scheme applied to both a telephone transaction corpus and an Enron email corpus, representing interactions as Dialogue Function Units with explicit links between requests and responses. It trains classifiers to predict relations between unit pairs and permits unmatched requests, making it directly relevant to localized response scope in workplace email.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2009"}
{"title":"Detection of Question-Answer Pairs in Email Conversations","url":"https://aclanthology.org/C04-1128/","doi":"10.3115/1220355.1220483","authors":["Lokesh Shrestha","Kathleen McKeown"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Develops methods for detecting questions and their corresponding answer segments in email threads. Thread-structure features improve question-answer pairing beyond lexical similarity, and the work explicitly motivates distinguishing questions about different issues and associating responses with the appropriate questions.","venue":"COLING 2004"}
{"title":"Question and Response Dynamics in Public Service Encounters","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.lrec-1.223/","doi":"10.63317/44ysuy55vyoi","authors":["Wassiliki Siskou","Ingrid Espinoza","Laurin Friedrich","Steffen Eckhard","Annette Hautli-Janisz"],"year":2026,"abstract":"Provides more than 200 verbatim transcripts of authentic German conversations between street-level bureaucrats and citizens together with annotations of all question-response pairs. The study distinguishes response behavior including direct and more evasive responses in an authentic institutional setting.","venue":"LREC 2026"}
{"title":"‘But what is the reason why you know such things?’: Question and response patterns in the LADO interview","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378216617301285","doi":"10.1016/j.pragma.2018.03.015","authors":["Alison Channon","Paul Foulkes","Traci Sue Walker"],"year":2018,"published":"2018-03-30","abstract":"Analyzes question-response pairs in five authentic institutional interviews. Directives frequently occur within multiple-question turns, and interviewees typically answer the most specific or most recent question rather than satisfying the accompanying directive, providing direct empirical evidence of partial response scope over multi-question antecedents.","venue":"Journal of Pragmatics"}
{"title":"Improving Dialogue Discourse Parsing via Reply-to Structures of Addressee Recognition","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.526/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.emnlp-main.526","authors":["Yaxin Fan","Feng Jiang","Peifeng Li","Fang Kong","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Models dialogue discourse structure by establishing discourse links and exploits addressee-recognition reply-to structures as a related source of structural supervision. A task-aware framework learns the shared and distinct structure of reply-to and discourse links on STAC and Molweni.","venue":"EMNLP 2023"}
{"title":"Structured Dialogue Discourse Parsing","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.sigdial-1.32/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.sigdial-1.32","authors":["Ta-Chung Chi","Alexander Rudnicky"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Treats dialogue discourse parsing as finding discourse links and corresponding relations throughout multi-participant conversations. The method jointly optimizes structural links and relations and performs structured decoding rather than independently assigning local pairwise labels.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2022"}
{"title":"A Distance-Aware Multi-Task Framework for Conversational Discourse Parsing","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.coling-1.76/","authors":["Yaxin Fan","Peifeng Li","Fang Kong","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Constructs utterance dependency structures for multi-party conversations while combining graph-based and transition-based parsing. The framework is specifically designed to improve both short- and long-distance discourse links, which is relevant when a response targets a non-adjacent antecedent.","venue":"COLING 2022"}
{"title":"Speaker-Aware Discourse Parsing on Multi-Party Dialogues","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.coling-1.477/","authors":["Nan Yu","Guohong Fu","Min Zhang"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Develops a discourse parser for multi-party dialogue that explicitly models interactions among speakers and predicts dialogue discourse structure. Speaker-context interaction and same-speaker pretraining improve structural link prediction on standard dialogue benchmarks.","venue":"COLING 2022"}
{"title":"Improving Multi-Party Dialogue Discourse Parsing via Domain Integration","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.codi-main.11/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.codi-main.11","authors":["Zhengyuan Liu","Nancy Chen"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Predicts dependency structures and discourse relations between elementary discourse units in multi-party conversations. It studies cross-domain generalization and domain integration for dialogue discourse-link prediction.","venue":"CODI 2021"}
{"title":"Dialogue Discourse Parsing as Generation: A Sequence-to-Sequence LLM-based Approach","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.sigdial-1.1/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.sigdial-1.1","authors":["Chuyuan Li","Yuwei Yin","Giuseppe Carenini"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Recasts dialogue discourse parsing as a text-to-text generation task so that a sequence-to-sequence language model generates discourse structures directly. The approach is evaluated on STAC and Molweni, where the target representation contains dialogue discourse links and relations.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2024"}
{"title":"Coherence-based Dialogue Discourse Structure Extraction using Open-Source Large Language Models","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.sigdial-1.26/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.sigdial-1.26","authors":["Gaetano Cimino","Chuyuan Li","Giuseppe Carenini","Vincenzo Deufemia"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Investigates unsupervised extraction of discourse structures from multi-party dialogue by identifying coherent sub-dialogues. Dynamic-programming and greedy algorithms are evaluated on STAC and Molweni without requiring gold discourse attachments during training.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2024"}
{"title":"Enhancing Multi-party Dialogue Discourse Parsing with Explanation Generation","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.103/","authors":["Shannan Liu","Peifeng Li","Yaxin Fan","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Combines LLM-generated explanatory information with structural parsing and contrastive learning for multi-party dialogue discourse parsing. The model targets discourse structure rather than merely classifying the current utterance and is evaluated on STAC and Molweni.","venue":"COLING 2025"}
{"title":"Improving Dialogue Discourse Parsing through Discourse-aware Utterance Clarification","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.920/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.920","authors":["Yaxin Fan","Peifeng Li","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Introduces a clarification module that reasons about ambiguous dialogue utterances before discourse parsing, including reasoning about the intended discourse relation. The parser evaluates whether clarifications improve structural predictions and is tested on STAC and Molweni.","venue":"ACL 2025"}
{"title":"An Annotation Scheme of A Large-scale Multi-party Dialogues Dataset for Discourse Parsing and Machine Comprehension","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.03514","arxiv_id":"1911.03514","authors":["Jiaqi Li","Ming Liu","Bing Qin","Zihao Zheng","Ting Liu"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Proposes an annotation scheme for large-scale multi-party Ubuntu chats containing explicit discourse structure together with question-answer information. The representation supplies discourse links among conversational units rather than only message-level reply structure."}
{"title":"Molweni: A Challenge Multiparty Dialogue-based Machine Reading Comprehension Dataset with Discourse Structure","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.coling-main.238/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.coling-main.238","authors":["Jiaqi Li","Ming Liu","Min-Yen Kan","Zihao Zheng","Zekun Wang","Wenqiang Lei","Ting Liu","Bing Qin"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Provides a large multiparty-dialogue corpus with discourse dependency annotations in an SDRT-style representation. It also includes answerable and unanswerable reading-comprehension questions, while the discourse annotations support explicit conversational attachment modeling.","venue":"COLING 2020"}
{"title":"A Joint Model for Dropped Pronoun Recovery and Conversational Discourse Parsing in Chinese Conversational Speech","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.138/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.acl-long.138","authors":["Jingxuan Yang","Kerui Xu","Jun Xu","Si Li","Sheng Gao","Jun Guo","Nianwen Xue","Ji-Rong Wen"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Jointly models dropped-pronoun recovery and conversational discourse parsing. Its discourse component constructs a conversational discourse graph with a biaffine link predictor, supplying transferable machinery for antecedent-link discovery in dialogue.","venue":"ACL-IJCNLP 2021"}
{"title":"Discourse Dependency Structures as Constrained DAGs","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W04-2324/","authors":["Laurence Danlos"],"year":2004,"venue":"SIGDIAL 2004"}
{"title":"Toward Dialogue Modeling: A Semantic Annotation Scheme for Questions and Answers","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W19-4027/","doi":"10.18653/v1/W19-4027","authors":["María Andrea Cruz Blandón","Gosse Minnema","Aria Nourbakhsh","Maria Boritchev","Maxime Amblard"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Proposes detailed annotations for both the semantic content and discourse contribution of question-answer pairs. The scheme is applied to dialogues in English, Spanish, and Dutch and is accompanied by initial automatic-annotation experiments.","venue":"Linguistic Annotation Workshop 2019"}
{"title":"Characterizing the Response Space of Questions: a Corpus Study for English and Polish","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W19-5937/","doi":"10.18653/v1/W19-5937","authors":["Jonathan Ginzburg","Zulipiye Yusupujiang","Chuyuan Li","Kexin Ren","Paweł Łupkowski"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Develops a fine-grained taxonomy intended to cover the full response space of questions rather than only direct answers. The taxonomy is grounded in corpus studies of English dialogue corpora and compared with Polish conversational data.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2019"}
{"title":"Characterizing the Response Space of Questions: data and theory","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.dnd-13.1/","doi":"10.5210/dad.2022.203","authors":["Jonathan Ginzburg","Zulipiye Yusupujiang","Chuyuan Li","Kexin Ren","Aleksandra Kucharska","Paweł Łupkowski"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Extends a corpus-grounded taxonomy to cover diverse responses to questions across several dialogue corpora and languages. It reports annotation reliability and formalizes response classes in a dialogical semantics, providing empirical distinctions beyond simple answer/non-answer labels.","venue":"Dialogue & Discourse"}
{"title":"UgChDial: A Uyghur Chat-based Dialogue Corpus for Response Space Classification","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.lrec-1.336/","authors":["Zulipiye Yusupujiang","Jonathan Ginzburg"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Introduces two-party and multi-party chat data containing explicitly identified question-response pairs. The pairs are annotated using a fine-grained response-space taxonomy, providing chat-based empirical evidence about different ways a question can be responded to.","venue":"LREC 2022"}
{"title":"A corpus-based taxonomy of question responses","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W13-0209/","authors":["Paweł Łupkowski","Jonathan Ginzburg"],"year":2013,"abstract":"Uses corpus evidence to develop a taxonomy of responses to questions rather than treating all following turns as equivalent answers. The taxonomy is intended to support formal and computational models of dialogue response behavior.","venue":"IWCS 2013"}
{"title":"Query responses","url":"https://jlm.ipipan.waw.pl/index.php/JLM/article/view/173","doi":"10.15398/jlm.v4i2.173","authors":["Paweł Łupkowski","Jonathan Ginzburg"],"year":2017,"published":"2017-05-22","abstract":"Presents a large-scale corpus study of cases where a query is answered with another query. It develops a response taxonomy and shows that response categories differ in how often a subsequent answer is eventually provided, offering evidence for tracking unresolved questions across turns.","venue":"Journal of Language Modelling"}
{"title":"Answering Clarification Questions","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W03-2103/","authors":["Matthew Purver","Patrick G.T. Healey","James King","Jonathan Ginzburg","Greg J. Mills"],"year":2003,"abstract":"Investigates clarification questions and their answers using corpus and experimental evidence. It examines relationships among clarification-request type, whether and how the clarification is answered, and the distance between request and answer.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2003"}
{"title":"Asking between the lines: Elicitation of evoked questions in text","url":"https://www.research.ed.ac.uk/en/publications/asking-between-the-lines-elicitation-of-evoked-questions-in-text","authors":["Matthijs Westera","Hannah Rohde"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Introduces a scalable method for eliciting Questions Under Discussion from naturalistic discourse and subsequently determining which elicited questions become answered as the discourse continues. This provides transferable annotation machinery for explicit unresolved-question tracking.","venue":"Amsterdam Colloquium 2019"}
{"title":"Discourse Analysis via Questions and Answers: Parsing Dependency Structures of Questions Under Discussion","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.710/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.findings-acl.710","authors":["Wei-Jen Ko","Yating Wu","Cutter Dalton","Dananjay Srinivas","Greg Durrett","Junyi Jessy Li"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Builds a QUD parser that derives dependency structures over full documents, treating later sentences as answers to questions evoked by preceding context. The approach is transferable to proposition-level antecedent discovery because the question itself represents the relation connecting a response to prior discourse.","venue":"Findings of ACL 2023"}
{"title":"Towards automatically generating Questions under Discussion to link information and discourse structure","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.coling-main.509/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.coling-main.509","authors":["Kordula De Kuthy","Madeeswaran Kannan","Haemanth Santhi Ponnusamy","Detmar Meurers"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Automates part of QUD annotation by generating potentially relevant questions for assertions in authentic discourse. The work is explicitly designed to link information structure with discourse structure and therefore supplies transferable machinery for representing proposition-to-question dependencies.","venue":"COLING 2020"}
{"title":"Inferring Acceptance and Rejection in Dialogue by Default Rules of Inference","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/cmp-lg/9609002","arxiv_id":"cmp-lg/9609002","doi":"10.1177/002383099603900306","authors":["Marilyn A. Walker"],"year":1996,"abstract":"Analyzes how dialogue participants infer whether assertions and proposals have been accepted or rejected. The account combines logical consistency, information structure and prosody, and develops a common-ground model covering several forms of acceptance and rejection.","venue":"Language and Speech"}
{"title":"Identifying Agreement and Disagreement in Conversational Speech: Use of Bayesian Networks to Model Pragmatic Dependencies","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P04-1085/","doi":"10.3115/1218955.1219040","authors":["Michel Galley","Kathleen McKeown","Julia Hirschberg","Elizabeth Shriberg"],"year":2004,"abstract":"First identifies adjacency pairs by ranking candidate utterance pairs using lexical, durational and structural features, and then classifies the linked pair as agreement or disagreement. Because antecedent-pair discovery precedes stance classification, the work directly addresses agreement/disagreement target selection rather than assuming a fixed target.","venue":"ACL 2004"}
{"title":"Conversational Grounding: Annotation and Analysis of Grounding Acts and Grounding Units","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16609","arxiv_id":"2403.16609","authors":["Biswesh Mohapatra","Seemab Hassan","Laurent Romary","Justine Cassell"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Annotates two dialogue corpora with Grounding Acts, Grounding Units and degree of grounding. Grounding Units explicitly organize sequences of conversational acts involved in establishing shared understanding, providing a structured representation relevant to acceptance and response scope.","venue":"LREC-COLING 2024"}
{"title":"A \"speech acts\" approach to grounding in conversation","url":"https://www.isca-archive.org/icslp_1992/traum92_icslp.html","doi":"10.21437/ICSLP.1992-41","authors":["David R. Traum","James F. Allen"],"year":1992,"abstract":"Models grounding as actions performed by conversational participants to establish mutual understanding. It defines Grounding Acts and a grammar specifying sequences of such acts that result in content becoming grounded.","venue":"ICSLP 1992"}
{"title":"Contributing to discourse","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1207/s15516709cog1302_7","doi":"10.1207/s15516709cog1302_7","authors":["Herbert H. Clark","Edward F. Schaefer"],"year":1989,"abstract":"Argues that discourse contributions require interlocutors to establish mutual belief that an utterance has been understood sufficiently for current purposes. It develops conversational contribution units and a collaborative account of how material enters common ground.","venue":"Cognitive Science"}
{"title":"Opening Up Closings","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/semi.1973.8.4.289","doi":"10.1515/semi.1973.8.4.289","authors":["Emanuel A. Schegloff","Harvey Sacks"],"year":1973,"abstract":"Provides a foundational conversation-analytic study of how conversational sequences are organized and brought to closure. Its analysis of sequential organization helped establish the interactional framework from which adjacency-pair and conditional-relevance accounts of responses developed.","venue":"Semiotica"}
{"title":"Discourse Deixis: Reference to Discourse Segments","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P88-1014/","doi":"10.3115/982023.982037","authors":["Bonnie Lynn Webber"],"year":1988,"abstract":"Develops an account of discourse-deictic reference in which expressions can refer to portions of prior discourse and to discourse-model objects evoked by those segments. It is foundational for treating antecedents as discourse segments rather than only nominal entities.","venue":"ACL 1988"}
{"title":"Resolving Individual and Abstract Anaphora in Texts and Dialogues","url":"https://aclanthology.org/C04-1034/","authors":["Costanza Navarretta"],"year":2004,"venue":"COLING 2004"}
{"title":"Annotating Abstract Pronominal Anaphora in the DAD Project","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L08-1331/","authors":["Costanza Navarretta","Sussi Olsen"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Extends an anaphora annotation scheme to abstract anaphora whose antecedents can be verbal phrases, clauses and discourse segments. Inter-annotator results on texts and dialogues in Danish and Italian indicate that these abstract-antecedent distinctions can be annotated reliably.","venue":"LREC 2008"}
{"title":"The DAD Parallel Corpora and their Uses","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L10-1222/","authors":["Costanza Navarretta"],"year":2010,"abstract":"Describes abstract-anaphora annotations in parallel and comparable written and spoken corpora. Antecedents include verbal phrases, clauses and discourse segments, while the referenced abstract objects include events, situations and propositions; the paper also reports machine-learning experiments on the annotations.","venue":"LREC 2010"}
{"title":"Anaphoric Annotation in the ARRAU Corpus","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L08-1091/","authors":["Massimo Poesio","Ron Artstein"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Introduces an anaphorically annotated corpus that explicitly represents multiple antecedents for ambiguous anaphoric expressions and discourse antecedents for references to abstract entities such as events, actions and plans. Its genres include task-oriented dialogues, making the representation especially relevant to ambiguity and multiple valid antecedents.","venue":"LREC 2008"}
{"title":"Resolving abstract anaphors in Spanish discourse: Underspecification and mereological structures","url":"https://scholarworks.indianapolis.iu.edu/items/565d6b77-ed93-4303-a752-1daaf06bbd37","doi":"10.1515/ling-2018-0008","authors":["Iker Zulaica Hernández"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Studies abstract anaphora where candidate antecedents are clauses, sentences and larger discourse fragments and the ambiguity occurs at the propositional level. It proposes constructing a complex abstract object with a mereological structure so that an anaphor can resolve to an atomic proposition or to a merged interpretation of multiple thematically connected propositions.","venue":"Linguistics"}
{"title":"Scoring Coreference Chains with Split-Antecedent Anaphors","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.dnd-14.3/","doi":"10.5210/dad.2023.201","authors":["Silviu Paun","Juntao Yu","Nafise Sadat Moosavi","Massimo Poesio"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Generalizes coreference evaluation metrics to references whose interpretation is constructed from multiple antecedents. The scoring approach has been applied both to split-antecedent plural references and to discourse deixis in dialogue, providing evaluation machinery for genuine multi-antecedent structures.","venue":"Dialogue & Discourse"}
===END llm_candidates.jsonl===
